1
00:00:00,433 --> 00:00:03,616
<i>A bus on a highway plunged off a bridge.
Twelve people died.</i>

2
00:00:03,616 --> 00:00:06,944
<i>In addition, the whereabouts of
a man who was on the bus are unknown.</i>

3
00:00:06,944 --> 00:00:10,400
Your husband was
having an affair with my wife.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,180
They were sitting next to each other.

5
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:14,660
I'm sorry.

6
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:18,400
Your height is just about the same too.

7
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,200
[Nagano Prefectural Police]

8
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:46,433
Hello?

9
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:49,933
Yes, that's right.

10
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:55,067
Yes.

11
00:00:58,467 --> 00:01:00,367
What?

12
00:01:01,967 --> 00:01:04,067
Yes, please.

13
00:01:04,067 --> 00:01:06,100
Excuse me.

14
00:01:12,267 --> 00:01:15,433
They say the search has been called off.

15
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:17,167
What? But that means...

16
00:01:17,167 --> 00:01:22,067
It seems Mitsuru wasn't on that bus.

17
00:01:23,233 --> 00:01:24,933
What?

18
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,767
Please take a look at this.

19
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:37,400
The man who just boarded.

20
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:39,633
Yes, that's my husband.

21
00:01:41,833 --> 00:01:46,200
Here, the same man is getting off the bus.

22
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,733
What?

23
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:51,756
Right after this, the bus departed
without your husband returning.

24
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:58,733
So in other words, my husband wasn't
on the bus at the time of the accident.

25
00:01:58,733 --> 00:02:02,867
He left his belongings behind,
and he seemed to be in a hurry.

26
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:05,967
I imagine there must have been some reason.

27
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:07,767
Before he returned to the bus the first time,

28
00:02:07,767 --> 00:02:12,533
we have security camera footage of him
shopping at a store in the service area.

29
00:02:12,533 --> 00:02:15,733
There was nothing particularly unusual
about his behavior.

30
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:18,433
He got off by himself.

31
00:02:19,467 --> 00:02:21,733
Why?

32
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:25,533
Like, he was trying to save only himself?

33
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:30,933
Because the accident happened
right after that.

34
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:33,780
Are you saying Mitsuru killed them? How?

35
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:37,933
No, no, I'm not saying that. Just...
I just thought it looked that way.

36
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:43,733
Anyway, since we now know
he got off the bus voluntarily,

37
00:02:43,733 --> 00:02:47,100
the search around the accident site
will be suspended.

38
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:48,567
Okay.

39
00:02:48,567 --> 00:02:51,400
But you will still search for him, right?

40
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,267
Like, canvassing the service area?

41
00:02:53,267 --> 00:02:57,000
As there is no evidence of a crime,
we cannot conduct an active search.

42
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,333
But he hasn't come home.

43
00:03:01,333 --> 00:03:03,933
He might have collapsed somewhere.

44
00:03:03,933 --> 00:03:06,444
Could he have gone missing?

45
00:03:07,967 --> 00:03:09,500
Missing?

46
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:12,567
It happens a lot.
Running away from the wife...

47
00:03:12,567 --> 00:03:17,233
People who disappear
to escape their current lives.

48
00:03:19,700 --> 00:03:23,567
We'll take your statement,
so please wait here.

49
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:25,400
Excuse me.

50
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:33,660
Sorry.

51
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:34,667
Well, don't be.

52
00:03:34,667 --> 00:03:38,140
It's not like he was trying
to save himself. I'm really sorry.

53
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,860
No, no.

54
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:50,467
Shall we rent a car nearby?

55
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:54,967
- A rental car?
- To go to the service area.

56
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:57,567
I'd really rather not take a bus.

57
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:05,267
Besides, your husband is probably alive.

58
00:04:05,267 --> 00:04:07,467
We have to look for him.

59
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,500
Azusa's death was an accident
caused by the driver's sudden illness.

60
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:18,700
It's frustrating, but I've accepted it.

61
00:04:19,633 --> 00:04:23,480
But why was she on that bus?

62
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:26,267
And why did the person
she was with disappear?

63
00:04:26,267 --> 00:04:30,567
I want a proper explanation for that too.

64
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,300
Right now, I want to meet your husband
more than you want to meet him.

65
00:04:38,067 --> 00:04:41,967
Even if you do get to meet him,
please don't hit him right away.

66
00:04:41,967 --> 00:04:45,067
- No, I might hit him.
- You can't.

67
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:47,580
I get to go first.

68
00:04:47,580 --> 00:04:50,400
Oh, so that's what you meant.

69
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,200
I definitely want to see him
more than you do.

70
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:58,167
Well, then... okay. Anyway...

71
00:05:07,008 --> 00:05:08,467
[Episode 3]

72
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:09,960
- Here.
- Okay, thank you very much.

73
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:12,720
- Take care, then. Have a safe trip.
- Thanks.

74
00:05:23,367 --> 00:05:25,267
What's wrong?

75
00:05:25,267 --> 00:05:27,480
Oh, nothing.

76
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:29,520
Can I make a quick call?

77
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:31,220
Sure.

78
00:05:37,567 --> 00:05:40,267
Hello, Naoto?

79
00:05:40,267 --> 00:05:42,700
I see.

80
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:44,500
That's good.

81
00:05:45,333 --> 00:05:47,667
Well, maybe not entirely good.

82
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:50,567
<i>It's hard to know how to react. Sorry.</i>

83
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:54,260
<i>But the chances that
he's still alive have gone way up,</i>

84
00:05:54,260 --> 00:05:57,020
<i>so I wanted to let you know.</i>

85
00:05:57,800 --> 00:05:59,767
Thank you.

86
00:05:59,767 --> 00:06:02,360
Let me know if you hear from Mitsuru.

87
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:06,633
<i>If he were able to contact me,
he'd reach out to you first.</i>

88
00:06:06,633 --> 00:06:08,480
That's not necessarily true.

89
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:15,280
<i>Mitsuru doesn't always
prioritize me over everything.</i>

90
00:06:16,333 --> 00:06:19,333
I'll contact you right away
if anything comes up.

91
00:06:19,333 --> 00:06:21,060
Thanks.

92
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,733
<i>Are you doing okay, Sakiko?</i>

93
00:06:23,733 --> 00:06:25,433
<i>Are you eating properly?</i>

94
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:27,633
Mrs. Seo.

95
00:06:27,633 --> 00:06:29,067
<i>I've set the GPS.</i>

96
00:06:29,067 --> 00:06:31,488
Shall we get going?

97
00:06:31,488 --> 00:06:33,233
<i>Please.</i>

98
00:06:34,048 --> 00:06:37,167
<i>Sorry. What did you just say?</i>

99
00:06:37,167 --> 00:06:38,433
Going for a drive?

100
00:06:38,433 --> 00:06:40,300
<i>Oh, no, no.</i>

101
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:43,767
I thought I'd go to the service area
where Mitsuru got off the bus.

102
00:06:45,267 --> 00:06:47,300
Please be careful.

103
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:50,233
Thanks. Bye.

104
00:06:50,233 --> 00:06:51,900
Okay.

105
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:59,733
Seriously, be careful.

106
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:17,400
- Sorry.
- Oh, no problem.

107
00:07:19,100 --> 00:07:21,300
What happened to the phone?

108
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:23,400
- Your mean my wife's phone?
- Yes.

109
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:26,833
It's broken and won't turn on.

110
00:07:26,833 --> 00:07:28,760
Aren't you getting it fixed?

111
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:32,533
To look for information inside?

112
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:39,867
Did your husband have his phone with him?

113
00:07:39,867 --> 00:07:42,967
His phone was on the bus too, apparently.

114
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:47,533
It's completely shattered and won't power on.

115
00:07:47,533 --> 00:07:50,067
Aren't you getting it fixed?

116
00:07:55,433 --> 00:08:00,433
About his phone, I'm scared of
what I might find.

117
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,900
And even if there's nothing,
I'd still feel guilty...

118
00:08:02,900 --> 00:08:04,800
I understand.

119
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:07,440
I was a little relieved
when the phone wouldn't turn on.

120
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,767
Right?

121
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:14,267
But things that
people themselves leave behind

122
00:08:14,267 --> 00:08:16,600
are more credible, in a way.

123
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:21,333
What? So you don't trust people's testimony?

124
00:08:21,333 --> 00:08:27,467
What Naoto said still
doesn't quite sit right with me.

125
00:08:27,467 --> 00:08:31,133
I can't imagine Mitsuru complaining about me.

126
00:08:32,067 --> 00:08:34,267
That's a lot of confidence.

127
00:08:34,267 --> 00:08:37,140
I'm sure he had some complaints,

128
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:41,733
but I just don't think he'd vent to Naoto.

129
00:08:41,733 --> 00:08:42,833
Why?

130
00:08:42,833 --> 00:08:47,267
Mitsuru is incredibly considerate
of other people's feelings.

131
00:08:47,267 --> 00:08:49,400
He's the employer, after all.

132
00:08:49,400 --> 00:08:54,733
He thinks that even the smallest thing that
makes someone uncomfortable is harassment.

133
00:08:54,733 --> 00:09:00,000
So no matter how dissatisfied
he might be with me,

134
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,533
I really don't think he'd complain to Naoto.

135
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:07,340
That's impressive.

136
00:09:08,133 --> 00:09:10,656
Well, he is impressive.

137
00:09:10,656 --> 00:09:14,124
He's always thinking about other people.

138
00:09:14,752 --> 00:09:16,060
I respect him.

139
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:18,100
No. What I mean is,

140
00:09:18,100 --> 00:09:23,700
can you really praise him so much
and trust him so unconditionally?

141
00:09:26,233 --> 00:09:30,633
Do you understand the situation?
He wasn't on the bus.

142
00:09:30,633 --> 00:09:33,824
And yet he hasn't come home.
That's beyond an affair...

143
00:09:33,824 --> 00:09:36,364
So he ran away from his wife?

144
00:09:42,633 --> 00:09:45,633
Why do you think I'm talking so freely?

145
00:09:45,633 --> 00:09:49,200
Because if I stop thinking,
I feel like I'll lose my mind.

146
00:09:50,633 --> 00:09:53,867
Even so, I believe in Mitsuru.

147
00:09:53,867 --> 00:09:55,940
Believe in which part?

148
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:59,300
That he didn't have an affair?
Or that he didn't run away?

149
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:01,196
Both.

150
00:10:02,833 --> 00:10:08,433
Because Mitsuru proposed to me.

151
00:10:09,067 --> 00:10:10,924
He asked me to marry him.

152
00:10:12,608 --> 00:10:13,964
What's that got to do with it?

153
00:10:31,552 --> 00:10:34,833
Oh, I've been here before.

154
00:10:35,567 --> 00:10:38,900
Did you come here
with the person who proposed to you?

155
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,767
What? How did you know?

156
00:10:40,767 --> 00:10:43,167
Well, just by the look on your face.

157
00:10:44,667 --> 00:10:46,336
My face?

158
00:10:46,336 --> 00:10:51,300
Which store was he seen shopping at
on the security camera?

159
00:10:52,633 --> 00:10:56,467
Apparently, he bought two curry buns
at a curry bun shop.

160
00:10:56,467 --> 00:10:58,367
Shall we go there?

161
00:10:58,367 --> 00:11:00,600
Do you have a photo of him?

162
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,233
Oh, right.

163
00:11:02,933 --> 00:11:06,500
How about this one?
It's from about two months ago.

164
00:11:08,167 --> 00:11:10,167
Or maybe this one?

165
00:11:10,167 --> 00:11:14,067
- Something like this.
- I think the first one is fine.

166
00:11:14,067 --> 00:11:15,800
Okay.

167
00:11:20,128 --> 00:11:22,067
Is this it?

168
00:11:25,856 --> 00:11:28,267
There's only one thing on the menu.

169
00:11:29,467 --> 00:11:32,467
Want anything to eat? I think I'll have one.

170
00:11:34,944 --> 00:11:36,167
[Smiling Curry Buns]

171
00:11:36,167 --> 00:11:37,933
Mrs. Seo?

172
00:11:41,233 --> 00:11:43,867
I'll have one too.

173
00:11:43,867 --> 00:11:47,133
Well, I suppose this isn't surprising.

174
00:11:47,133 --> 00:11:52,267
Hundreds of customers come here every day.
There's no way they'd remember a face.

175
00:11:52,267 --> 00:11:54,000
What should we do?

176
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:58,033
Should we ask around
at any of the other stores?

177
00:12:01,667 --> 00:12:03,867
Are you listening?

178
00:12:06,867 --> 00:12:08,800
This.

179
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,300
I ate this with Mitsuru.

180
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:14,000
What?

181
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,160
Pumpkin.

182
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:16,867
Oh. That's true, there's pumpkin in it.

183
00:12:16,867 --> 00:12:19,167
Did Azusa like pumpkin?

184
00:12:19,167 --> 00:12:21,824
Yes, she did.

185
00:12:21,824 --> 00:12:23,867
I see.

186
00:12:23,867 --> 00:12:25,600
Yeah.

187
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,233
We ate it together,

188
00:12:28,233 --> 00:12:31,820
and I remember making a fuss,
saying, "Wow, there's pumpkin in it!"

189
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:34,267
What? You don't like it?

190
00:12:35,833 --> 00:12:38,067
I like it.

191
00:12:38,067 --> 00:12:40,267
But Mitsuru doesn't.

192
00:12:41,568 --> 00:12:43,800
Then why did he buy it again?

193
00:12:44,667 --> 00:12:47,200
Maybe for Azusa?

194
00:12:50,367 --> 00:12:55,167
I kept saying it was really delicious,
and I even gobbled up Mitsuru's too.

195
00:12:55,167 --> 00:13:00,267
He probably remembered that these are
delicious for people who like pumpkin.

196
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:04,067
But he bought two, right?

197
00:13:04,067 --> 00:13:07,233
Maybe he just forgot
that it had pumpkin in it?

198
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:10,000
I see.

199
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:15,980
Maybe he just bought one
for himself and one for Azusa,

200
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:21,260
or maybe he forgot
that he'd eaten it with me.

201
00:13:26,933 --> 00:13:29,772
That was delicious.
I think I'll buy some to take home.

202
00:13:39,367 --> 00:13:42,633
Sorry for leaving you here
to return the car by yourself.

203
00:13:42,633 --> 00:13:46,867
No, it's fine. I'm going to drive it
to my parents' place now.

204
00:13:46,867 --> 00:13:48,833
Oh, that's good.

205
00:13:49,767 --> 00:13:51,800
Does Sho like pumpkin?

206
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:53,400
Huh? Yes, he does...

207
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:57,300
- Then this is a souvenir for him.
- No, no. Wasn't it for yourself?

208
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,933
I'd be very happy as long as
someone who likes pumpkin gets to eat it.

209
00:14:00,933 --> 00:14:02,688
- All right, thank you.
- You're welcome.

210
00:14:02,688 --> 00:14:05,667
Well,

211
00:14:05,667 --> 00:14:09,440
I'll contact you if I find out
anything from the phone.

212
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:10,967
What?

213
00:14:10,967 --> 00:14:13,020
I'll get it fixed.

214
00:14:14,167 --> 00:14:15,744
Don't worry, I won't
force you to do the same.

215
00:14:15,744 --> 00:14:17,100
Oh, okay.

216
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:22,967
- All right. Thank you very much.
- Oh, not at all.

217
00:14:22,967 --> 00:14:24,733
Take care.

218
00:14:27,904 --> 00:14:29,484
You too.

219
00:15:05,200 --> 00:15:07,067
Sorry.

220
00:15:08,067 --> 00:15:11,433
A car driven by a man,

221
00:15:12,133 --> 00:15:14,767
and I got in the passenger seat.

222
00:15:14,767 --> 00:15:16,740
If you'd sat in the back,

223
00:15:18,680 --> 00:15:21,280
it would seem like you were being reserved,

224
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:24,033
and you thought that might make
the driver feel awkward, right?

225
00:15:24,867 --> 00:15:26,467
Yes.

226
00:15:27,167 --> 00:15:29,820
I hesitated when getting in.

227
00:15:29,820 --> 00:15:33,433
So kind, always thinking about others.

228
00:15:35,233 --> 00:15:37,700
Do you actually like pumpkin?

229
00:15:37,700 --> 00:15:39,980
No, I don't.

230
00:15:40,833 --> 00:15:42,600
That's what I thought.

231
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:44,633
A while ago,

232
00:15:46,233 --> 00:15:50,600
when there was about one serving
of curry left in the pot,

233
00:15:50,600 --> 00:15:57,833
I microwaved some frozen bite-sized pumpkin
and added it to the curry.

234
00:15:57,833 --> 00:16:01,533
I didn't know that. Was it good?

235
00:16:01,533 --> 00:16:03,333
Yes, it was good.

236
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:06,900
It was good,

237
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:11,867
but I felt rushed to finish it
before you came home.

238
00:16:11,867 --> 00:16:13,500
Why?

239
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,733
You weren't doing anything wrong.

240
00:16:16,967 --> 00:16:21,700
But I secretly made it and secretly ate it.

241
00:16:22,733 --> 00:16:28,100
Because it's something I like,
but that you dislike.

242
00:16:30,533 --> 00:16:34,333
To make it a flavor
that would suit both of us,

243
00:16:34,333 --> 00:16:39,100
it was a curry we made
together and ate together,

244
00:16:41,033 --> 00:16:43,628
and that's what I did with the leftovers.

245
00:16:46,333 --> 00:16:48,867
It's not wrong,

246
00:16:50,533 --> 00:16:52,800
but I felt like hiding it.

247
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:04,076
When you were meeting Azusa every month,

248
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,400
was that the same feeling?

249
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:15,564
Oh dear.

250
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:38,800
- Sho.
- Daddy.

251
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:44,780
Fist bump! Touch! Squeeze!
Have you been a good boy?

252
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:46,467
- Yeah.
- Good.

253
00:17:46,467 --> 00:17:49,360
- Look.
- What's this?

254
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:53,100
Wait, that's not the one
I gave you for your birthday.

255
00:17:53,100 --> 00:17:55,967
I got it for being a good boy.

256
00:17:58,600 --> 00:18:00,700
Thank you.

257
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:02,667
Even with everything
that's going on with Dad.

258
00:18:02,667 --> 00:18:04,500
Not at all.

259
00:18:04,500 --> 00:18:07,200
Daddy, when will we be
coming back home again?

260
00:18:07,200 --> 00:18:11,233
Home? But home is our house
in Tokyo, you know? Oh, Sho...

261
00:18:11,233 --> 00:18:14,967
I drove here today.
Want to go home together by car?

262
00:18:14,967 --> 00:18:17,367
No, you can't ride in a car when
you don't know who else has been in it.

263
00:18:17,367 --> 00:18:19,740
Sho, let's go give you a bath.

264
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:22,900
- Can you get your pajamas ready?
- Sure.

265
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:30,167
Don't try to buy his affection.

266
00:18:30,167 --> 00:18:32,380
Azusa didn't like that either, you know?

267
00:18:32,380 --> 00:18:36,333
But Azusa was oddly strict with him.
Don't you feel for poor Sho?

268
00:18:36,333 --> 00:18:38,333
It's normal discipline.

269
00:18:38,333 --> 00:18:40,320
Every family is like that.

270
00:18:40,320 --> 00:18:42,048
She doesn't know what "normal" is.

271
00:18:42,048 --> 00:18:44,300
Because she wasn't raised in a normal family.

272
00:18:45,300 --> 00:18:47,533
- About that...
- Huh?

273
00:18:47,533 --> 00:18:51,333
About Azusa's upbringing...
Did you ever say anything about it to her face?

274
00:18:52,833 --> 00:18:53,900
I wonder if I did.

275
00:18:53,900 --> 00:18:57,233
- But maybe that comes out subconsciously...
- What's that? A souvenir?

276
00:18:57,233 --> 00:19:01,460
Huh? No, I told you, it's for Sho.
Someone gave me some curry buns.

277
00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:04,233
Did you get them
from that woman you went out with?

278
00:19:04,233 --> 00:19:05,767
What?

279
00:19:05,767 --> 00:19:10,067
You have to be careful with cars.
A woman's scent can linger.

280
00:19:10,067 --> 00:19:12,200
She's not that kind of person.

281
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:17,000
Giving greasy food like this
to a five-year-old?

282
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,567
That person must not have kids.

283
00:19:19,567 --> 00:19:21,967
Well, maybe this is a good thing.
You could consider remarrying.

284
00:19:21,967 --> 00:19:23,840
Enough.

285
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:26,200
- Ta-da!
- Whoa!

286
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:28,033
Sho.

287
00:19:28,033 --> 00:19:31,400
When should we go back
to our home in Tokyo? Tomorrow?

288
00:19:31,400 --> 00:19:34,333
Will Mama come home tomorrow?

289
00:19:34,333 --> 00:19:37,800
Probably not tomorrow.

290
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:39,667
Then I think I'll stay here a little longer.

291
00:19:39,667 --> 00:19:41,300
Really?

292
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:58,333
[Sakiko Seo: Thank you for accompanying me
today. I'm grateful. No need to reply]

293
00:20:13,167 --> 00:20:14,900
- Yo!
- Hey.

294
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:17,967
- Is your son still at your parents' place?
- Ah, yeah.

295
00:20:17,967 --> 00:20:21,767
Instead of going back there on weekends,
you should take the kid out somewhere.

296
00:20:21,767 --> 00:20:23,780
Before the grandma
encroaching her grandchild life.

297
00:20:23,780 --> 00:20:24,967
True.

298
00:20:24,967 --> 00:20:26,440
- He's five, right?
- Yeah.

299
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:29,260
It's too hot outside right now,
so I'd recommend somewhere indoors.

300
00:20:29,260 --> 00:20:31,467
What indoor places are there
that would be fun?

301
00:20:31,467 --> 00:20:36,800
KidZania, Anpanman Children's Museum,
Sanrio Puroland...

302
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:39,633
Wait, let me write that down.

303
00:20:39,633 --> 00:20:45,733
- Okay, KidZania, Anpanman...
- And an aquarium is a safe choice.

304
00:20:49,233 --> 00:20:52,833
KidZania, Anpanman...
and... what was the other one?

305
00:20:52,833 --> 00:20:54,143
- Puroland.
- Puroland...

306
00:20:54,143 --> 00:20:56,260
Oh, the Hello Kitty one... That's indoors?

307
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:59,733
Yeah. But I'd still recommend the aquarium.

308
00:20:59,733 --> 00:21:02,060
It's relaxing for adults too,
and there's one near a train station.

309
00:21:03,936 --> 00:21:06,580
I've never been to KidZania. Sounds fun.

310
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:10,467
Sorry to keep you waiting.

311
00:21:11,167 --> 00:21:13,067
Here it is.

312
00:21:14,567 --> 00:21:17,267
Oh, sorry about that...

313
00:21:19,267 --> 00:21:21,367
What about the password?

314
00:21:21,367 --> 00:21:24,333
We can't unlock it here.

315
00:21:25,233 --> 00:21:27,200
I see.

316
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:53,167
Let's try birthdays first.

317
00:21:53,167 --> 00:21:55,833
[Entering password]

318
00:22:00,667 --> 00:22:02,900
Scary...

319
00:22:06,500 --> 00:22:08,900
Such a hurdle.

320
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:13,133
I tried birthdays
and our wedding anniversary, but no luck.

321
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:16,567
- What about you?
- Huh?

322
00:22:16,567 --> 00:22:19,067
What's your phone password?

323
00:22:20,267 --> 00:22:21,933
What?

324
00:22:22,700 --> 00:22:25,900
I won't look.

325
00:22:25,900 --> 00:22:30,667
I'm just gathering information on what
criteria men use to choose a four-digit code.

326
00:22:30,667 --> 00:22:34,600
It's Sho's birth weight. 3075 grams.

327
00:22:34,600 --> 00:22:38,400
- Oh, I said it.
- Huh, so it's not birthdays, then.

328
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:42,267
Well, birthdays are easy
for others to find out.

329
00:22:42,267 --> 00:22:45,000
Birth weight isn't so obvious.

330
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:49,580
I see. But parents would know.

331
00:22:49,580 --> 00:22:52,800
I think Azusa could have easily unlocked it.

332
00:22:52,800 --> 00:22:55,267
That means you have nothing to hide.

333
00:22:56,833 --> 00:23:00,600
Yes, it's also a way of showing that.

334
00:23:01,420 --> 00:23:02,567
Showing?

335
00:23:02,567 --> 00:23:06,140
A password that my wife can easily guess.

336
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:09,467
You know. It looks like
I'm not hiding anything, right?

337
00:23:09,467 --> 00:23:12,533
And it's a number related to our child.

338
00:23:12,533 --> 00:23:14,633
Huh...

339
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:18,667
Which means...

340
00:23:19,533 --> 00:23:23,267
Mitsuru really is...

341
00:23:27,167 --> 00:23:31,133
You've really lost a lot of your confidence.

342
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:34,333
You were so sure before.

343
00:23:34,333 --> 00:23:36,500
Saying it was because he proposed to you.

344
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:38,800
With a reason that made no sense.

345
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:41,500
You believed so purely and innocently.

346
00:23:46,700 --> 00:23:48,767
I'm sorry.

347
00:23:50,133 --> 00:23:53,833
I know it's presumptuous of me to believe,

348
00:23:54,733 --> 00:23:57,800
and from your perspective,

349
00:23:57,800 --> 00:24:00,800
it must seem selfish.

350
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:04,672
I still don't know the circumstances,

351
00:24:04,672 --> 00:24:08,373
but it's true they were together,

352
00:24:09,728 --> 00:24:11,916
and Mitsuru was the only one who survived.

353
00:24:17,067 --> 00:24:18,572
I'm sorry.

354
00:24:20,433 --> 00:24:23,052
No, you don't need to apologize for that.

355
00:24:24,767 --> 00:24:26,800
I'm sorry.

356
00:24:31,533 --> 00:24:35,533
Let's find him
and make him apologize himself.

357
00:24:35,533 --> 00:24:37,833
Okay? What should we do next?

358
00:24:37,833 --> 00:24:40,333
Maybe put up posters?

359
00:24:40,333 --> 00:24:43,367
- Like for lost pets. I see those often...
- I'm sorry.

360
00:24:47,800 --> 00:24:53,667
I do feel like saying
"What the hell, man?" to Mitsuru.

361
00:24:53,667 --> 00:24:57,000
But you haven't done
anything wrong, Mrs. Seo.

362
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,900
- There's no need for you to apologize.
- We're family.

363
00:25:01,933 --> 00:25:03,733
I'm his wife.

364
00:25:04,600 --> 00:25:06,633
I have responsibility.

365
00:25:09,667 --> 00:25:13,772
Well... I had a college classmate

366
00:25:15,167 --> 00:25:19,500
who struggled with job hunting
because his brother had a criminal record.

367
00:25:20,367 --> 00:25:22,533
I thought that was strange,

368
00:25:22,533 --> 00:25:25,867
because he's a good guy, and he cut ties
with his elder brother who did drugs.

369
00:25:25,867 --> 00:25:27,916
Of course, he never did drugs himself.

370
00:25:29,400 --> 00:25:33,167
Well... maybe the situation is different,

371
00:25:33,167 --> 00:25:38,167
but anyway, there's no reason
to take out any anger at Mitsuru on you.

372
00:25:41,467 --> 00:25:45,068
When we find him, you can go first.

373
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:49,267
You mean hitting him?
Do you want me to go first?

374
00:25:49,267 --> 00:25:50,400
Yes.

375
00:25:50,400 --> 00:25:53,360
Suddenly, I'm feeling motivated.

376
00:25:53,360 --> 00:25:55,833
I'll find him, come hell or high water.

377
00:25:56,500 --> 00:25:58,733
Okay.

378
00:25:58,733 --> 00:26:00,933
What about you, Mrs. Seo?

379
00:26:00,933 --> 00:26:03,167
What's your password?

380
00:26:03,167 --> 00:26:05,267
Come on, I told you mine.

381
00:26:05,267 --> 00:26:07,467
Lay it bare.

382
00:26:07,467 --> 00:26:09,567
0317.

383
00:26:10,300 --> 00:26:12,733
Mitsuru's birthday.

384
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:17,900
You really love him, huh?

385
00:26:17,900 --> 00:26:22,000
How to search for a missing person.

386
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:26,467
Police... Oh, right, private detectives...

387
00:26:27,296 --> 00:26:29,548
Social media... Whoa!

388
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:51,967
What are you going to do without me?
Can you get stains out?

389
00:26:51,967 --> 00:26:53,967
No.

390
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,133
- The stained part, though.
- Yeah.

391
00:26:57,133 --> 00:26:59,600
- Just cut it out like this.
- Oh?

392
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:02,272
Maybe make it
a sexy peek-a-boo look or something?

393
00:27:02,272 --> 00:27:05,200
Sorry, I have no idea
what you're talking about.

394
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:07,116
This here.

395
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:10,700
This T-shirt you gave me, Azusa.

396
00:27:10,700 --> 00:27:12,600
It's insanely good.

397
00:27:12,600 --> 00:27:15,500
You're the most particular man
about white T-shirts in all of Japan.

398
00:27:15,500 --> 00:27:17,260
This one really is perfect.

399
00:27:18,033 --> 00:27:20,967
The size too.

400
00:27:20,967 --> 00:27:24,433
The fabric thickness, the neckline tightness.

401
00:27:24,433 --> 00:27:26,080
- Right?
- Where did you buy it?

402
00:27:26,080 --> 00:27:30,400
Online. You can only buy it
from the official online shop.

403
00:27:30,400 --> 00:27:32,800
It takes quite a while
from ordering to delivery.

404
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:34,200
Oh, one of those.

405
00:27:34,200 --> 00:27:35,948
Should I buy them in bulk?

406
00:27:36,633 --> 00:27:38,092
Yeah.

407
00:27:41,633 --> 00:27:43,052
Yes?

408
00:27:50,833 --> 00:27:52,833
- Delivery for you.
- Oh, thanks.

409
00:27:52,833 --> 00:27:54,188
[Parcel for Azusa Sonoda]
Thank you.

410
00:28:06,133 --> 00:28:07,788
It's for you, Azusa.

411
00:28:11,733 --> 00:28:13,420
What did you buy?

412
00:28:26,933 --> 00:28:28,556
I'm opening it.

413
00:29:02,688 --> 00:29:05,292
Sorry, it's your favorite.

414
00:29:18,767 --> 00:29:22,760
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival:
Sky in Bloom on a Cool Evening 2025]

415
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:24,800
Fireworks festival.

416
00:29:30,304 --> 00:29:33,516
[Couple's Seat]

417
00:29:39,520 --> 00:29:41,612
- Good morning.
- Morning.

418
00:29:45,088 --> 00:29:47,667
- Mrs. Chizuru.
- Yes?

419
00:29:47,667 --> 00:29:52,900
I think I'd like you to trust me
with a full-fledged project soon.

420
00:29:52,900 --> 00:29:54,180
I'm bored.

421
00:29:54,180 --> 00:29:57,380
Everyone else seems busy,
and I feel bad about it.

422
00:30:01,500 --> 00:30:03,340
If that's what you want, Sakiko.

423
00:30:04,133 --> 00:30:05,660
Thank you.

424
00:30:05,660 --> 00:30:09,233
It's okay. He'll definitely come back.

425
00:30:09,233 --> 00:30:10,580
Yeah.

426
00:30:10,580 --> 00:30:12,500
- Oh, Rino.
- Yes?

427
00:30:12,500 --> 00:30:15,700
I have tickets to a fireworks festival.
Want to go with your boyfriend?

428
00:30:15,700 --> 00:30:18,833
- They're something called couple's seats.
- Really?

429
00:30:18,833 --> 00:30:21,000
I'd love to go!

430
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:22,720
- Great.
- Here you go.

431
00:30:22,720 --> 00:30:24,428
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival]
[Couple's Seat]

432
00:30:25,700 --> 00:30:28,100
Ah, sorry.

433
00:30:28,100 --> 00:30:30,200
I have a prior engagement that day.

434
00:30:30,200 --> 00:30:32,233
- Sorry.
- I see.

435
00:30:32,233 --> 00:30:33,767
Okay.

436
00:30:33,767 --> 00:30:36,844
Why do you have tickets
to a fireworks show, Sakiko?

437
00:30:37,967 --> 00:30:40,864
They fell out of Mitsuru's jacket.

438
00:30:40,864 --> 00:30:41,933
What?

439
00:30:41,933 --> 00:30:45,200
Two tickets for next month's
fireworks festival.

440
00:30:45,200 --> 00:30:47,067
Couple's seats.

441
00:30:47,067 --> 00:30:50,333
Really?

442
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:53,767
- Hey.
- Yes?

443
00:30:53,767 --> 00:30:58,033
Where do you hide things
you don't want your wife to see?

444
00:30:58,033 --> 00:31:00,233
I don't have anything
I'd be in trouble for if she saw.

445
00:31:00,233 --> 00:31:03,767
- I see.
- Why?

446
00:31:06,112 --> 00:31:07,564
Okay then...

447
00:31:08,667 --> 00:31:11,533
What if you wanted to go
somewhere your wife doesn't like?

448
00:31:11,533 --> 00:31:13,388
A place that's hard to go to alone.

449
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:18,800
- I'd go with you, Chizuru.
- Of course you would.

450
00:31:18,800 --> 00:31:21,000
That's no help at all.

451
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:25,800
For all those 'normal' couples out there,

452
00:31:25,800 --> 00:31:29,467
doesn't doing things secretly
make your relationship worse?

453
00:31:31,133 --> 00:31:33,196
I see.

454
00:31:41,533 --> 00:31:45,733
Just that last bit is fine, Sakiko,
so no need to do any more.

455
00:31:45,733 --> 00:31:48,033
Okay, then I'll stop here.

456
00:31:48,033 --> 00:31:49,900
Thank you.

457
00:31:50,733 --> 00:31:53,600
I suppose you can't just hire
part-time staff on your own.

458
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:55,833
Exactly.

459
00:31:56,667 --> 00:31:58,340
But maybe I should hire someone.

460
00:31:58,340 --> 00:32:00,033
Go for it.

461
00:32:00,033 --> 00:32:02,433
Just take over the shop.

462
00:32:07,833 --> 00:32:09,932
Sorry about my husband.

463
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:15,600
Marriage is tough, isn't it?

464
00:32:15,600 --> 00:32:17,708
Even though you've done
nothing wrong, Sakiko.

465
00:32:18,333 --> 00:32:21,484
It can't be helped. We're family.

466
00:32:23,333 --> 00:32:26,444
Family? But aren't you a married couple?

467
00:32:27,067 --> 00:32:28,933
Yeah.

468
00:32:28,933 --> 00:32:33,200
What? A married couple is family.

469
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:35,367
But originally you were strangers.

470
00:32:35,367 --> 00:32:38,800
But now we're married.

471
00:32:38,800 --> 00:32:41,033
We're not strangers anymore.

472
00:32:46,633 --> 00:32:49,836
Oh, the signboard for
the restaurant is still outside.

473
00:32:57,533 --> 00:32:59,767
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

474
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:02,867
Hello?

475
00:33:10,333 --> 00:33:12,000
Huh?

476
00:33:12,633 --> 00:33:14,500
They hung up.

477
00:33:16,300 --> 00:33:19,067
It happens sometimes. Prank calls.

478
00:33:19,067 --> 00:33:21,452
What? That's not very nice.

479
00:33:31,700 --> 00:33:34,660
- Naoto.
- Yes?

480
00:33:34,660 --> 00:33:37,233
Can I take Mitsuru's apron home?

481
00:33:37,233 --> 00:33:38,700
I want to wash it.

482
00:34:00,064 --> 00:34:01,472
Where's the little one?

483
00:34:01,472 --> 00:34:04,767
Oh, my mom is looking after him
at my parents' place.

484
00:34:04,767 --> 00:34:06,867
I see.

485
00:34:07,567 --> 00:34:11,116
Sounds like she's supportive.
That's a good family.

486
00:34:19,600 --> 00:34:23,467
This is quite cute, isn't it?

487
00:34:23,467 --> 00:34:28,000
Azusa gave it to me.

488
00:34:28,833 --> 00:34:32,492
She said, "Old folks can die from the heat."

489
00:34:34,016 --> 00:34:38,960
I noticed that Azusa's was different,
so I thought hers had broken,

490
00:34:38,960 --> 00:34:41,004
but she said she'd given it away.

491
00:34:42,067 --> 00:34:43,533
Well, I ended up getting it.

492
00:34:43,533 --> 00:34:45,772
Oh, I see.

493
00:34:49,533 --> 00:34:50,933
Here, take this.

494
00:34:50,933 --> 00:34:55,633
I brought your wife's belongings
that were left at the shop.

495
00:34:55,633 --> 00:34:59,533
- Oh, right. Sorry for the trouble.
- No, not at all.

496
00:34:59,533 --> 00:35:01,120
I should have taken them home last time.

497
00:35:01,120 --> 00:35:04,500
No, no, I hadn't tidied up yet last time.

498
00:35:04,500 --> 00:35:07,328
And... you know,

499
00:35:07,328 --> 00:35:10,892
I also need to sort out my feelings.

500
00:35:14,567 --> 00:35:17,836
[Shinagawa Aquarium]

501
00:35:22,300 --> 00:35:24,367
The aquarium.

502
00:35:26,467 --> 00:35:30,660
Azusa really liked aquariums.

503
00:35:30,660 --> 00:35:35,967
She was always looking up
different ones during her breaks.

504
00:35:41,567 --> 00:35:44,033
- What? Is something weird?
- Huh?

505
00:35:44,033 --> 00:35:45,700
- No.
- No.

506
00:35:45,700 --> 00:35:48,700
Is it weird for a girl
to want to go to an aquarium?

507
00:35:48,700 --> 00:35:49,933
No.

508
00:35:49,933 --> 00:35:52,700
Oh, I get it. Because there are two tickets.

509
00:35:52,700 --> 00:35:57,633
Well, she probably meant
to go with you, right?

510
00:35:57,633 --> 00:35:59,300
Or maybe with your little one?

511
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:00,167
Well, I don't know.

512
00:36:00,167 --> 00:36:03,233
- Sometimes just you two, like a date...
- Oh, do you want tea?

513
00:36:03,233 --> 00:36:05,804
- Or would you prefer coffee?
- Yeah, sure.

514
00:36:11,900 --> 00:36:14,508
About this little fan...

515
00:36:15,133 --> 00:36:17,300
Yes?

516
00:36:17,300 --> 00:36:23,000
Why didn't you ask her who she gave it to?
Weren't you curious?

517
00:36:23,000 --> 00:36:26,800
No, my wife tells me
what she wants to on her own.

518
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:31,567
You don't know how she is at home.

519
00:36:31,567 --> 00:36:34,200
Every family and couple
has their own dynamic.

520
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:37,960
No, no, of course. Sorry, I was just a bit...

521
00:36:37,960 --> 00:36:40,000
I was being a bit too nosy.

522
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:43,700
My wife's catchphrase at home was
"Hey Itsuki, listen to this."

523
00:36:43,700 --> 00:36:46,040
When I got home first,

524
00:36:46,040 --> 00:36:48,500
she'd start talking
before even closing the front door.

525
00:36:48,500 --> 00:36:51,033
- About anything and everything.
- I see.

526
00:36:51,033 --> 00:36:52,800
She did often tell me things after the fact,

527
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:56,200
and I'd be like, "You went there?"
or "You bought that?"

528
00:36:56,200 --> 00:37:00,100
But that spontaneous
and free-spirited side of her,

529
00:37:00,100 --> 00:37:02,060
that was Azusa at home.

530
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:04,267
Yeah, that sounds about right.

531
00:37:04,267 --> 00:37:06,200
That day too,

532
00:37:06,200 --> 00:37:08,733
I'm sure this is what she would have told me
when she got home.

533
00:37:08,733 --> 00:37:10,733
"Hey Itsuki, listen to this.

534
00:37:10,733 --> 00:37:14,563
Today, I went to Nagano by bus with Mr. Seo,

535
00:37:14,563 --> 00:37:18,304
the husband of the lady who lives nearby."

536
00:37:18,304 --> 00:37:19,633
And then?

537
00:37:19,633 --> 00:37:24,700
"I met Mr. Seo about six months ago
at the laundromat."

538
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:28,400
We often chat while waiting for our laundry.

539
00:37:28,400 --> 00:37:30,767
I told you about that before, didn't I?

540
00:37:30,767 --> 00:37:33,867
That delicious soba shop in Nagano
that we saw on Brunch.

541
00:37:33,867 --> 00:37:35,467
When I mentioned it to Mr. Seo,

542
00:37:35,467 --> 00:37:39,500
he said, "Oh, I've been
wanting to go there too."

543
00:37:39,500 --> 00:37:42,933
And I was like, "Really?
Then let's go together!"

544
00:37:45,733 --> 00:37:47,424
Sorry.

545
00:37:47,424 --> 00:37:53,533
I wanted to get away from Tokyo,
be in nature, and relax for once.

546
00:37:53,533 --> 00:37:57,060
I meant to tell you properly before I left,

547
00:37:57,060 --> 00:38:00,867
but Miki was on her way over
to play with Sho,

548
00:38:00,867 --> 00:38:05,233
and going out with a man at that time...

549
00:38:05,233 --> 00:38:09,467
I didn't want your mom to get the wrong idea.

550
00:38:09,467 --> 00:38:11,400
I overthought things.

551
00:38:11,400 --> 00:38:13,800
So I'm telling you after the fact.

552
00:38:14,900 --> 00:38:16,400
Oh, but,

553
00:38:16,400 --> 00:38:19,667
even though he's a man,
there's really nothing between us.

554
00:38:19,667 --> 00:38:22,100
We just chat once a month at the laundromat.

555
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:24,733
Today was the first time
we met outside of that.

556
00:38:24,733 --> 00:38:28,700
- But we sat next to each other on the bus...
- <i>"But we sat next to each other on the bus...</i>

557
00:38:28,700 --> 00:38:31,200
- I'm really sorry about that.
- <i>I'm really sorry about that.</i>

558
00:38:31,200 --> 00:38:35,367
I figured you'd believe me, Itsuki,
so I'm telling you the truth.

559
00:38:35,367 --> 00:38:37,533
You do believe me, right?"

560
00:38:39,467 --> 00:38:42,200
That's how she would have told me.

561
00:38:42,200 --> 00:38:44,256
Well...

562
00:38:44,256 --> 00:38:48,133
- Wonderful. Thanks. That was very interesting.
- Thank you.

563
00:38:49,067 --> 00:38:52,067
Is barley tea okay?

564
00:38:52,067 --> 00:38:53,820
Well, then...

565
00:38:53,820 --> 00:38:57,933
Well, I take back what I said earlier.

566
00:38:57,933 --> 00:39:03,033
Because, you know, just for
the convenience of those left behind,

567
00:39:03,033 --> 00:39:05,700
twisting a person's past is

568
00:39:05,700 --> 00:39:09,967
not right, I thought. So... I'll say it.

569
00:39:10,592 --> 00:39:11,600
All right.

570
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:16,967
We often chatted during work.

571
00:39:18,333 --> 00:39:21,100
At that time...

572
00:39:21,100 --> 00:39:23,733
Her catchphrase was...

573
00:39:25,033 --> 00:39:27,667
"Hey, sir,

574
00:39:27,667 --> 00:39:30,933
listen to something I can't tell my husband."

575
00:39:30,933 --> 00:39:32,700
That was it.

576
00:39:33,867 --> 00:39:35,567
That's all.

577
00:39:45,167 --> 00:39:48,200
- All right, I'll take my leave now. Bye.
- Oh...

578
00:39:57,100 --> 00:39:59,367
<i>Taking advantage of the fact that she's dead,</i>

579
00:39:59,367 --> 00:40:04,333
<i>I might be idealizing
both myself and Azusa too much.</i>

580
00:40:05,278 --> 00:40:07,852
[Shinagawa Aquarium]

581
00:40:09,433 --> 00:40:12,833
<i>I absolutely hate aquariums.</i>

582
00:40:20,833 --> 00:40:24,933
<i>And Azusa knows that very well.</i>

583
00:40:32,500 --> 00:40:36,033
<i>I absolutely hate fireworks festivals.</i>

584
00:40:46,500 --> 00:40:51,133
<i>And Mitsuru knows that very well.</i>

585
00:41:00,367 --> 00:41:03,700
Maki's getting married.

586
00:41:10,816 --> 00:41:13,500
The wedding...

587
00:41:14,304 --> 00:41:17,228
June 28th...

588
00:41:43,967 --> 00:41:46,800
<i>Checking out his phone without permission,</i>

589
00:41:46,800 --> 00:41:49,568
<i>I'd feel guilty about it
when Mitsuru came back.</i>

590
00:41:49,568 --> 00:41:53,068
[48th Musashino Summer Fireworks Festival]

591
00:42:10,867 --> 00:42:13,200
<i>But it's okay now.</i>

592
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,433
<i>Mitsuru probably...</i>

593
00:42:17,100 --> 00:42:19,433
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

594
00:42:19,433 --> 00:42:21,633
<i>won't come back.</i>

595
00:42:21,633 --> 00:42:23,600
Hello?

596
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:25,500
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

597
00:42:29,900 --> 00:42:32,544
It happens sometimes. Prank calls.

598
00:42:32,544 --> 00:42:35,267
What? That's not very nice.

599
00:42:40,533 --> 00:42:45,484
<i>Mitsuru isn't coming back anymore.</i>

600
00:42:51,467 --> 00:42:56,588
She apparently went to the service area.
With that husband too.

601
00:42:57,700 --> 00:43:00,140
So she's fine.

602
00:43:00,864 --> 00:43:04,267
She seems to be able to go to work too.

603
00:43:04,267 --> 00:43:10,067
Yesterday, though it was accidental,
wasn't it good to hear her voice a little?

604
00:43:10,067 --> 00:43:12,633
I was a bit on edge, though.

605
00:43:15,367 --> 00:43:19,767
If you're that worried, just come back,

606
00:43:19,767 --> 00:43:21,700
Mr. Seo.

